\title{Explicit Sections and Persistent Collisions\\in Iterated Collatz Maps}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 25, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We examine unrestricted iterated Collatz maps as candidates for one-way functions and collision-resistant hashing. For nonnegative integers, let $T(n)=n/2$ when $n$ is even and $T(n)=3n+1$ when $n$ is odd, and let $F_a=T^a$. Every $F_a$ has an explicit efficiently computable section: $I_a(y)=2^a y$, with $F_a(I_a(y))=y$ for every target. Thus unrestricted inversion requires no branching search. Independently, the distinct inputs $2k+1$ and $12k+8$ have the same one-step image $6k+4$ for every $k\geq0$. Determinism propagates these collisions through every subsequent iterate. These unconditional facts remain true if Collatz convergence is assumed. We formulate the resulting cryptographic obstruction, give algorithms and complexity bounds, separate inversion from forward convergence, and identify bounded-domain and domain-separated variants that remain meaningful research directions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The Collatz map combines halving with an affine expansion selected by parity. Its rule is immediately computable, but its long-term dynamics resist complete analysis. This contrast suggests a cryptographic analogy: perhaps a long trajectory is easy to compute forward but difficult to reconstruct backward.

Such a proposal must be tested against the exact inversion relation. One-wayness is not the qualitative assertion that trajectories look irregular; it is an average-case claim over specified distributions and size parameters. Before asymptotic hardness is plausible, one must ask whether a structural inverse already exists. Likewise, collision resistance cannot arise from iteration if algebraically described collisions occur at the first step and persist forever.

On the unrestricted domain $\mathbb N=\{0,1,2,\ldots\}$, both obstructions occur. Every target has an all-even reverse path of arbitrary prescribed length, beginning at $2^a y$. Moreover,
\[
2k+1\neq12k+8,
\qquad T(2k+1)=T(12k+8)=6k+4.
\]
The first identity defeats unrestricted inversion; the second gives efficiently generated persistent collisions. Restricted domains may remove these shortcuts, but then define genuinely different problems.

\section{Definitions and computational setting}

\begin{definition}[Collatz map]
The Collatz map $T:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ is
\[
T(n)=\begin{cases}
n/2,&n\equiv0\pmod2,\\
3n+1,&n\equiv1\pmod2.
\end{cases}
\]
In particular, $T(0)=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Iterates]
Let $T^0$ be the identity and $T^{a+1}=T^a\circ T$. We write $F_a(n)=T^a(n)$ for the state after exactly $a$ steps.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Canonical preimage]
For $a,y\in\mathbb N$, set
\[
I_a(y)=2^a y.
\]
This is the point obtained by taking the reverse even edge $y\mapsto2y$ exactly $a$ times.
\end{definition}

For fixed $a$, unrestricted inversion asks for any $n\in\mathbb N$ satisfying $F_a(n)=y$. A function $I_a$ is a section, or right inverse, if $F_a(I_a(y))=y$ for every $y$. A collision is a pair $x\neq z$ with $F_a(x)=F_a(z)$.

Integers are represented in binary. If $\ell(n)$ denotes binary length, multiplying by $2^a$ is a left shift by $a$ places and produces at most $\ell(y)+a$ bits. Numeric exponential growth is therefore not exponential growth in representation length.

\section{The explicit section}

\begin{lemma}[Universal even predecessor]
For every $n\in\mathbb N$,
\[
T(2n)=n.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The input $2n$ is even, so the even branch gives $T(2n)=(2n)/2=n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact all-even preimage]
For all $a,y\in\mathbb N$,
\[
T^a(2^a y)=y.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $a$. For $a=0$ the assertion is $T^0(y)=y$. If it holds at depth $a$, then $2^{a+1}y=2(2^a y)$, so the first step gives $2^a y$. The remaining $a$ steps reach $y$ by induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Additive composition]
For all $a,b,y\in\mathbb N$,
\[
I_{a+b}(y)=I_a(I_b(y)).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The two sides are $2^{a+b}y$ and $2^a(2^b y)$, respectively, and are equal by the exponent law.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Section theorem]
For every $a\in\mathbb N$, $I_a$ is a right inverse of $F_a$:
\[
F_a\circ I_a=\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb N}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Evaluate at $y$ and apply the exact all-even preimage theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Surjectivity]
Every iterate $F_a$ is surjective.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Given $y$, choose $n=2^a y$. Then $F_a(n)=y$.
\end{proof}

Surjectivity alone is not a cryptographic attack: a permutation is surjective. The decisive property is the explicit and elementary section.

\section{Inversion algorithm and complexity}

\begin{algorithm}[Canonical all-even inverter]
Given depth $a\geq0$ and target $y\geq0$, return $n=2^a y$. In binary, append $a$ zero bits to $y$.
\end{algorithm}

For example, when $a=5$ and $y=13$, the output is $416$, and
\[
416\to208\to104\to52\to26\to13.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Total correctness]
The canonical all-even inverter terminates on every input and returns $n$ satisfying $F_a(n)=y$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The shift is finite, and correctness is the exact all-even preimage theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Bit complexity]
The inverter uses $O(\ell(y)+a)$ time and emits $O(\ell(y)+a)$ bits in the standard bit-cost model.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Copy the bits of $y$ and append $a$ zeros. This writes $\ell(y)+a$ bits, up to the fixed convention for zero.
\end{proof}

No reverse tree is searched. Therefore a lower bound such as $2^{a/\log a}$ cannot describe this unrestricted inversion relation.

\section{Parameterized persistent collisions}

\begin{theorem}[Parameterized one-step collisions]
For every $k\in\mathbb N$, let $x_k=2k+1$ and $z_k=12k+8$. Then $x_k\neq z_k$ and
\[
T(x_k)=T(z_k)=6k+4.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first input is odd, hence $T(x_k)=3(2k+1)+1=6k+4$. The second is even, hence $T(z_k)=(12k+8)/2=6k+4$. Equality of the inputs would imply $10k=-7$, impossible for $k\geq0$.
\end{proof}

The first examples are $T(1)=T(8)=4$, $T(3)=T(20)=10$, and $T(5)=T(32)=16$.

\begin{lemma}[Collision propagation]
If $T(x)=T(z)$, then for every $b\in\mathbb N$,
\[
T^{b+1}(x)=T^{b+1}(z).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Apply the deterministic function $T^b$ to both sides of $T(x)=T(z)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Collision at every positive depth]
For every $a>0$, distinct $x,z\in\mathbb N$ exist with $F_a(x)=F_a(z)$. In fact, $x=1$ and $z=8$ work for every positive $a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We have $T(1)=T(8)$. Write $a=b+1$ and apply collision propagation.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Noninjectivity]
No positive-depth iterate $F_a$ is injective.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The preceding theorem supplies two unequal inputs with equal images.
\end{proof}

The result is stronger than noninjectivity for cryptographic purposes. A collision can be generated from any $k$, and the same pair collides at every later depth.

\section{Combined cryptographic obstruction}

\begin{theorem}[Unrestricted cryptographic obstruction]
For every positive integer $a$, the map $F_a(n)=T^a(n)$ simultaneously satisfies:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item for every $y\in\mathbb N$, $F_a(2^a y)=y$;
\item distinct inputs collide, and one may always choose $1$ and $8$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Clause (i) is the exact all-even preimage theorem. Clause (ii) is the collision-at-every-depth theorem.
\end{proof}

Under the unrestricted task ``given $(a,y)$, find any $n$ with $T^a(n)=y$,'' inversion succeeds for every instance in output-linear time. Under the raw collision task ``given $a>0$, find distinct $x,z$ with $T^a(x)=T^a(z)$,'' the fixed pair $(1,8)$ always succeeds. These attacks require only a shift and fixed small integers.

\section{Independence from convergence}

\begin{definition}[Collatz convergence assertion]
Every positive integer eventually reaches $1$:
\[
\forall n>0\;\exists a\geq0\text{ such that }T^a(n)=1.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Convergence does not block inversion]
Even if the Collatz convergence assertion is assumed, for every $a,y\in\mathbb N$ there exists $n$ with $T^a(n)=y$. One may take $n=2^a y$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The exact all-even preimage identity proves the conclusion without any convergence assumption.
\end{proof}

Convergence concerns eventual forward behavior, with a depth that may depend on the starting point. Exact-depth inversion asks a different question. The deterministic reverse edge $y\mapsto2y$ remains available regardless of forward convergence.

\section{Scope, applications, and design principles}
The obstruction applies to the unrestricted natural-number domain, an inversion game accepting any preimage, and raw iterates without additional encoding. A length-preserving game may reject $2^a y$. If inputs must remain in $[2^{b-1},2^b)$, reverse odd steps and congruence constraints can become relevant. No average-case lower bound for that altered problem is asserted here.

One-wayness is distributional. A complete proposal must specify how inputs are sampled, how $a$ scales, how outputs are encoded, and what constitutes success. An attacker generally needs any accepted preimage, not necessarily the original input. Thus admissibility must be part of the definition.

Compression functions are necessarily noninjective, so noninjectivity alone does not rule out every hashing use. Here the stronger defect is an efficiently parameterized collision family stable under every further iterate. Any domain separator must remove the entire family, not merely small examples.

The analysis suggests two generic screening principles. First, search for efficiently computable sections $S_a$ satisfying $G_a(S_a(y))=y$ before treating orbit complexity as hardness. Second, search for stable kernel pairs: if $G(x)=G(z)$, then every deterministic suffix $H$ satisfies $H(G(x))=H(G(z))$. Efficiently parameterized pairs prevent iteration alone from creating collision resistance.

\section{Numerical demonstrations}
Three direct experiments illustrate the results. The first computes $2^a y$ and prints its all-even path to $y$. The second generates $(2k+1,12k+8)$ and confirms their common image $6k+4$. The third compares trajectories from $1$ and $8$ at arbitrary positive depth; after the first transition the paths coincide. These calculations illustrate universal algebraic identities rather than provide evidence from finite sampling.

\section{Future research}
\subsection{Length-preserving inversion}
Fix bit length $b$, sample $n$ uniformly from $[2^{b-1},2^b)$, reveal an encoding of $T^a(n)$, and require a preimage in the same interval. The universal preimage may then be inadmissible. A precise conjecture must specify the relation between $a$ and $b$ and an average-case computational model.

\subsection{Entropy of reverse parity words}
Reverse trajectories can be encoded by parity words, but odd reverse moves impose divisibility and parity congruences. Counting admissible bounded preimages may expose an exponential rate after the exceptional all-even word is excluded.

\subsection{Domain separation and compression}
Keyed affine separators and length-preserving compression rules should be screened against the full identity $T(2k+1)=T(12k+8)$ and its persistent descendants before stronger security claims are attempted.

\subsection{Generic obstruction theorems}
One may seek a theorem in a standard circuit model stating that efficiently computable sections defeat distribution-free unrestricted one-wayness, while efficiently parameterized, iteration-stable kernel pairs defeat collision resistance of raw iterates.

\section{Comparison with one-way-function requirements}
A conventional one-way-function claim includes a security parameter, a polynomial-time sampler, and a probability bound against every efficient probabilistic inverter. The unrestricted relation fails earlier: the inverter succeeds with probability one on every target whenever any natural-number preimage is accepted. No distribution rescues that exact relation.

A restricted relation can differ. If verification requires membership in $D_{a,b}$, the canonical attack applies only when $2^a y\in D_{a,b}$. Analysis must then characterize that event and alternative reverse paths. A complete definition should state the sampled domain, admissible answer set, scaling, representation, and success probability.

Collision resistance also normally concerns keyed finite-domain families. The raw iterates fail any natural game admitting $1$ and $8$. Excluding one pair is inadequate unless the full algebraic collision families and efficiently derived variants are addressed.

\section{Conclusion}
Every unrestricted iterated Collatz map has a total explicit section: at depth $a$, multiplication by $2^a$ supplies a preimage of every target and is merely a binary left shift. Independently, $(2k+1,12k+8)$ is an infinite family of one-step collisions, each persistent under further iteration. Both facts are independent of the Collatz convergence conjecture.

Dynamical unpredictability and cryptographic hardness are different properties. Before orbit complexity is used as a security resource, efficient sections, parameterized collisions, and encoding shortcuts must be excluded. Viable successors must therefore change the problem through domain bounds, distributional requirements, output encodings, or structural collision exclusion.

