The widget exhibits the identity $T^a(2^a y)=y$. The corresponding implementation and its output-sensitive complexity appear here:

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the proof of the exact-depth inversion theorem</summary>
Every number $y$ has the even predecessor $2y$, since $T(2y)=y$. Repeating this identity $a$ times yields the chain $2^a y,2^{a-1}y,\ldots,2y,y$. Induction on $a$ proves the statement for every nonnegative depth and target. In binary, forming the first term appends $a$ zero bits, so the computation is linear in its output length.
</details>

## Collisions are algebraic, not accidental

Now vary $k$. The odd input $2k+1$ and even input $12k+8$ are distinct but both map to $6k+4$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the collision calculation</summary>
For the odd branch, $3(2k+1)+1=6k+4$. For the even branch, $(12k+8)/2=6k+4$. Equality of the inputs would force $10k=-7$, impossible for $k\ge0$. Thus every nonnegative $k$ gives a genuine collision.
</details>

## Once merged, always merged

A deterministic function cannot separate equal states. The smallest collision, $T(1)=T(8)=4$, therefore survives every positive iteration depth.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the propagation argument and cryptographic consequence</summary>
If $T(x)=T(z)$, applying $T^b$ to both sides gives $T^{b+1}(x)=T^{b+1}(z)$. Consequently, $1$ and $8$ collide under every positive iterate. Together with the explicit section $y\mapsto2^a y$, this rules out unrestricted one-wayness and collision resistance for the raw iterate family. See the [general definition of a one-way function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-way_function) to compare the required computational game.
</details>

## Where a serious successor must begin

The obstruction does not analyze a same-bit-length preimage game, keyed domain separation, or a restricted sampling distribution. Those variants must explicitly exclude the all-even section and the known collision families. Their natural objects of study are admissible reverse parity words, congruence constraints, and average-case search within a bounded interval.
